# fix_pin ... from_pins -- consume the LEF pin PORTS the BDB now holds (schema v32).
#
# `import_def_lef` stores each signal pin's port rectangles (cell-local, with the
# layer the LEF drew them on).  `fix_pin cell:mac from_pins` then writes one
# per-pin cell fix from them, so the placement the LEF already states becomes the
# landing constraint without anyone retyping it:
#
#   a  west face, y 20..60, M6 (horizontal)  -> west at 20..60 layer M6
#   b  north face, x 30..70, M7 (vertical)   -> north at 30..70 layer M7
#   c  south face, x 20..80, M6 (horizontal) -> south at 20..80  (M6 runs the wrong
#                                               way for a south face, so no layer)
#   i  interior                              -> no boundary port, left free
#
# mac_b is placed turned 180 degrees: the SAME fixes land on its east / south /
# north faces (the cell-level transform of the previous change).
#
# Run:   bin/buda flow/pin_fix/from_pins.buda          (viewer; green = landings)
# Expect: the `[PinFix] mac from_pins: 3 pin(s) fixed from LEF ports, 1 interior ...`
#         line, `dump_pin_fixes` listing the three selectors, and check_design
#         Success at both stages.

source ../tracks/tracks.buda

corner_margin dx 5 dy 5
set_min_stub_length 2

open_bdb :memory:
import_def_lef from_pins.def from_pins.lef

fix_pin cell:mac from_pins
dump_pin_fixes

derive_busterms 0
add_blocks_from_bdb 0

run_hier_bundler depth 0
generate_hier_topologies
run_planner hier 5
run_nuts
check_design nuts
run_detailed_nuts
check_design dnuts

visualize
